==== Flashing via AirOS web gui ====
  - Downgrade airOS to 5.5 or lower via tftp or web and after proceed with the next steps\\ {{media:ubiquiti:nanom2-downgrade.png?600|Flashing via AirOS web gui}}
  - Once downgraded to 5.5, just go to update firmware and load openwrt-ar71xx-generic-ubnt-nano-m-squashfs-factory.bin image:\\ {{media:ubiquiti:nanostation_loco_m2.png?600|Flashing via AirOS web gui}}\\ and flash the OpenWrt firmware

==== Flashing via TFTP method ====
First you need to put Nanostation into TFTP server mode. Turn on Nanostation and keep reset button pressed for 20 seconds, until led light start flashing differently. When you see that odd and even wifi signal leds start turning on and off, you have put Nanostation in TFTP server mode.

Now you can send image from your PC to Nanostation. You need TFTP client that will send file to Nanostation which is running TFTP server. Server is running on 192.168.1.20 address by default.

If you are running Linux on your PC here is how you can send firmware image to Nanostation:
<code>ifconfig eth0 192.168.1.254
cp  openwrt-ar71xx-generic-ubnt-nano-m-squashfs-factory.bin code.bin
echo -e "binary\nrexmt 1\ntimeout 60\ntrace\nput code.bin flash_update\n" | tftp 192.168.1.20</code> 

==== Recover stock firmware flashed with airos 5.6 or higher ====
Requirements:
    * 1 USB-TTL Cable
    * 2 Ethernet Cables
    * 1 POE Converter
    * 1 Bricked Device
    * 1 TFTP Server Application (Example: Solar Winds)
    * 1 AirOS v5.5.10 Firmware File
    * Tera-term or another Serial Port program
 
The following is the Procedure to unbrick the device:
 
  - Open up the device and locate the serial jumper, labeled J1.
  - Connect the USB-TTL Cable to the jumper, which has the following pinout: 
    * '' 1 '' 3.3V+ (Red: Do not connect)
    * '' 2 '' S-in (GREEN)
    * '' 3 '' S-out (White)
    * '' 4 '' GND(Black)
  - Open up a terminal program, such as tera-term, and configure it to the following settings:
    * Bits per second: 115200
    * Data Bits : 8
    * Stop Bits : 1
    * Parity : None
    * Flow control : None
  - Plug the POE adapter into the wall.
  - Plug the LAN side of the device into your PC.
  - Configure your PC IP settings to the following:
    * IP Address: 192.168.1.123
    * Netmask : 255.255.0.0
  - Connect the POE side of the POE adapter into the Bullet M2 (or any other AirMax device).
  - Continually press any key while the tera-term window is selected. Doing this will interrupt the boot sequence and allow you to enter terminals while the device is in the bootloader.
  - Once the boot has been stopped, enter the following into the terminal: <code bash>urescue -f -e</code> <WRAP center round info 60%>
It is vital that the -f and -e are there. This allows the bootloader to be over written.
</WRAP>
  - Now start the TFTP application on your PC.
  - Open a command prompt on your PC and use the cd command to navigate to the location of your AirOS v5.5.10 firmware file.
  - Type the following command in the command prompt: <code bash>tftp -i 192.168.1.20 put XM.v5.5.10.24241.141001.1649.bin</code>
  - You should now see text being written in the terminal screen.
  - Keep watching the text until it says the device is resetting. When it does reset, continually press any key to interrupt the boot.
  - When the boot has been interrupted, type the following in the serial console, pressing enter after each line: <code bash>
 
mtdparts default

saveenv

boot</code>
  - The device will now boot. Wait about 2 minutes, and then access the webpage of AirOS by typing 192.168.1.20 in the web address bar.
  - Login to the page and navigate to the system tab. Upload the same firmware version, AirOS v5.5.10, that you used in the urescue command.
  - Once the device reboots you will have a non-bricked, perfectly functional, wireless device.


